3245
05:42:38,638 --> 05:42:46,432
It is called as warping. So basically, there is warpage of components at certain areas.

3246
05:42:46,702 --> 05:42:48,836
It usually happens at the corners.

3247
05:42:49,232 --> 05:42:52,992
Now all the 3D printing processes that we have learned.

3248
05:42:53,712 --> 05:42:55,012
Let's have a recap

3249
05:42:55,872 --> 05:43:01,312
on the very basic method of transforming material to some form.

3250
05:43:01,552 --> 05:43:04,356
It is done by using some form of energy.

3251
05:43:04,752 --> 05:43:08,612
Now that energy can be heat or that energy can be laser.

3252
05:43:09,232 --> 05:43:15,392
Now, in all the cases, any kind of energy which is given, basically it is resulting into heat.

3253
05:43:15,552 --> 05:43:26,084
So basically, any material from the raw material shape is heated to form the desired shape. Now, when it cools down, it shows some shrinkage.

3254
05:43:26,542 --> 05:43:30,672
And when it is shrinking, it also has some stresses.

3255
05:43:30,990 --> 05:43:38,592
Usually these stresses are very high at the corner points. That is why the parts they tend to

3256
05:43:39,822 --> 05:43:40,644
curl up.

3257
05:43:40,912 --> 05:43:49,712
Now, what is the basic cause of warping is that whenever you have poor adhesion to your base plate, when we are

3258
05:43:50,126 --> 05:43:52,912
printing FDM components, we are printing on a base plate.

3259
05:43:53,072 --> 05:43:55,140
Whenever the part is not

3260
05:43:57,568 --> 05:44:01,808
Stick properly to the base plate, the adhesion is not proper.

3261
05:44:02,048 --> 05:44:09,648
Then, because of these stresses, the thermal stresses, it may end up in curling up or warping.

3262
05:44:09,728 --> 05:44:17,284
So, warpage is a very basic technique, a very basic defect that we see due to thermal contraction.

3263
05:44:17,888 --> 05:44:22,244
Now, the very common FDM materials are ABS and nylon.

3264
05:44:22,448 --> 05:44:24,292
Now, while they shrink, they

3265
05:44:24,974 --> 05:44:26,500
show thermal contraction.

3266
05:44:26,928 --> 05:44:30,628
So, in almost all the FDM materials,

3267
05:44:31,790 --> 05:44:35,568
this phenomena is there, but in ABS and nylon it is slightly more.

3268
05:44:35,822 --> 05:44:46,148
So, when you are printing ABS and nylos, you need to make sure that your parts are adhered very in a very proper manner to the base plate so that they don't show any warpage.

3269
05:44:47,408 --> 05:44:53,412
Then another defect that you see on your screen is cracking.

3270
05:44:53,728 --> 05:45:02,368
Now, cracking is again a form of warpage or delamination, you can say, but it happens in between the parts.

3271
05:45:02,574 --> 05:45:06,208
So when two layers, they don't stick to each other.

3272
05:45:06,574 --> 05:45:10,148
what happens that? because of stresses, they show some,

3273
05:45:11,648 --> 05:45:12,528
I would say,

3274
05:45:14,048 --> 05:45:15,808
non-adherence.

3275
05:45:16,494 --> 05:45:23,204
they do not come in contact with each other. they tend to warp. and that's why we see such kind of cracking.

3276
05:45:23,408 --> 05:45:29,988
Now, cracking is again due to poor adhesion between layers and also thermal shrinkages.

3277
05:45:30,608 --> 05:45:35,780
So, most of the defects will be because of thermal shrinkages or thermal contraction.

3278
05:45:36,688 --> 05:45:39,204
Another defect in FDM is

3279
05:45:41,168 --> 05:45:42,048
stringing.

3280
05:45:42,222 --> 05:45:45,316
Now, stringing is basically when instead of

3281
05:45:47,790 --> 05:45:56,688
your nozzle moves from one feature to another to print that feature, it is not cooled down properly, and when it moves to the other feature,

3282
05:45:56,848 --> 05:46:02,208
it leaves a string of the material which is attached to the part as well as to the nozzle.

3283
05:46:03,854 --> 05:46:06,048
Now there can be multiple reasons.

3284
05:46:06,254 --> 05:46:14,928
One of the very basic reason for stringing is when the print temperature is too high, that means you are not giving proper cooling time to your nozzle

3285
05:46:15,150 --> 05:46:20,448
when it is and before cooling, it is moving from one place to another, causing the stringing effect.

3286
05:46:20,608 --> 05:46:30,448
So that is why you need to make sure that your print temperature is not very high and you are giving enough cooling time for the nozzle to move from one place to another.

3287
05:46:31,328 --> 05:46:35,808
Then again, one more effect, one more defect, this is curling.

3288
05:46:36,288 --> 05:46:46,208
Now, curling is slightly related to warpage, but curling will happen not because of thermal stresses but because of overheating of the material.

3289
05:46:46,448 --> 05:46:54,928
So when your print temperature is too high and your cooling is not happening, then your part will remain for a longer time in the molten

3290
05:46:55,168 --> 05:47:04,452
stage. And wherever it has remained for a longer time in the molten stage, what will happen that when you print on top of that, your printing is not proper.

3291
05:47:04,928 --> 05:47:07,140
So it will result in curling.

3292
05:47:08,848 --> 05:47:12,688
Now, let's talk about SLS and MJF. Again, this

3293
05:47:13,902 --> 05:47:20,128
defect that you see is from SLS technology and it is known as

3294
05:47:21,678 --> 05:47:22,928
delamination.

3295
05:47:23,168 --> 05:47:26,368
Now, delamination is again because of thermal stresses.

3296
05:47:26,608 --> 05:47:33,888
Now, all what happens that when two layers are not sintered or adhered to each other, then

3297
05:47:34,638 --> 05:47:42,688
because of the thermal stresses, when the stress is higher than the yield strength of the material, they tend to detach from each other.

3298
05:47:42,862 --> 05:47:48,848
These two layers do not attach to each other, instead, they detach from each other and thus resulting in delamination.

3299
05:47:49,008 --> 05:47:57,458
Now remember SLS, MJF, in MJF, what we do we use a curing head to provide heat to the sintered

3300
05:47:57,648 --> 05:48:05,168
part. Whereas in SLS we use a laser to sinter the component. So in both the cases, you will face

3301
05:48:05,968 --> 05:48:08,448
such delamination effects.

3302
05:48:09,486 --> 05:48:16,868
Then we have warping in SLS and MJF as well, and you can see here also the corners are lifted up.

3303
05:48:17,198 --> 05:48:26,928
So when the corners lift up, that means they have not been attached properly to the previous layer or the temperature is too high, and because of

3304
05:48:28,654 --> 05:48:33,888
straight portion that should be there in the part, it we are experiencing a curved portion.

3305
05:48:34,368 --> 05:48:34,688
So,

3306
05:48:35,968 --> 05:48:42,244
what we will see is that the part dimensions have deviated, what you have given in the model.

3307
05:48:42,510 --> 05:48:44,356
So, again, your part will be rejected.

3308
05:48:45,454 --> 05:48:56,144
Now, when we talk about LPF metal, again the reason for defects here is again the same improper cooling or excessive heating or high thermal stresses,

3309
05:48:56,304 --> 05:48:59,108
but the end effect might be completely different.

3310
05:48:59,534 --> 05:49:07,584
So, what happens in uh blazer powder bed fusion of metal, or we can say direct metal laser sintering that we studied?

3311
05:49:07,824 --> 05:49:11,344
That it is the laser is

3312
05:49:13,134 --> 05:49:15,364
melting the powder particles.

3313
05:49:16,078 --> 05:49:17,424
So when it

3314
05:49:18,304 --> 05:49:24,260
melts, there can be high energy which you are giving to melt, or there can be low energy.

3315
05:49:24,750 --> 05:49:31,904
You exactly need to give the appropriate amount of energy which will result into dense metal, but sometimes we don't get dense metal, we get porosity.

3316
05:49:31,984 --> 05:49:40,068
You can see different kind of porosity, regular and irregular. So, regular porosity, spherical porosity is when you have high energy input.

3317
05:49:40,430 --> 05:49:48,624
So, what happens that some of the alloying elements they evaporate in gaseous form, and when they evaporate in gaseous form, the these gaseous

3318
05:49:49,038 --> 05:49:52,516
gas particles, when they evaporate, they leave spherical force behind.

3319
05:49:53,184 --> 05:50:01,184
And when your energy is low, you are giving low energy input to melt the metal powder particles in LPBF,

3320
05:50:01,424 --> 05:50:06,464
then you will get irregular shape because that area represents unmelted powder.

3321
05:50:06,638 --> 05:50:13,344
And when this what you see on a screen is a microscopic study of a specimen made in LPBF.

3322
05:50:13,504 --> 05:50:23,556
So when you observe under microscope, you will see regular, irregular, irregular corresponds to insufficient melting, and regular corresponds to very high energy given during melting.

3323
05:50:24,878 --> 05:50:29,344
Then again, we can see delamination in metals also.

3324
05:50:29,584 --> 05:50:35,744
Now, what you see on your screen here we have support structures and then we have the part.

3325
05:50:36,224 --> 05:50:41,604
Now, when the thermal stresses are too high

3326
05:50:42,542 --> 05:50:50,784
and the bonding with the base plate or the support structure is not good enough, the metal part that you have built

3327
05:50:50,944 --> 05:50:53,860
that tends to delaminate from the support structures.

3328
05:50:55,150 --> 05:51:07,504
So, again, this is because of high stresses. So remember, most of the defects that we get in any of the 3D printing process is because of high stresses generated due to thermal contraction

3329
05:51:07,984 --> 05:51:14,532
because we are using thermal energy to transform the part into your desired shape.

3330
05:51:15,342 --> 05:51:20,388
So thank you, and we will meet in the next module and we will learn about the quality considerations.

3331
05:51:22,224 --> 05:51:34,464
Welcome back to the module quality considerations in 3D printing, and now we will learn about the various quality control measures that we can take in 3D printing.

3332
05:51:35,264 --> 05:51:35,584
So,

3333
05:51:37,134 --> 05:51:46,020
moving forward, uh, the first measure that we need to take as a quality control measure is design validation.

3334
05:51:46,624 --> 05:51:50,372
So, what you need to do is you need to validate your design.

3335
05:51:51,182 --> 05:51:55,972
Now, this design validation means also validating your design data.

3336
05:51:56,718 --> 05:52:00,384
There are multiple aspects of it, such as

3337
05:52:06,318 --> 05:52:14,224
features, you validating your tolerances, you validating your STL file, and you validating your minimum dimensions, critical dimensions.

3338
05:52:14,384 --> 05:52:22,624
For example, in a 3D printing process, you can print only 0.3 mm and you have a feature of 0.1 mm in your design. So definitely that design cannot be printed.

3339
05:52:22,784 --> 05:52:33,220
So, this design validation is a very important process that needs to be done during your designing process itself so that you can make design the right part for the right 3D printing process.

3340
05:52:33,824 --> 05:52:39,364
Then we talk about in process in situ process monitoring or in process monitoring.

3341
05:52:39,584 --> 05:52:47,364
So, basically, what you want to do is I told you that temperature can be one of the reasons for generating most of the defects. So when you are

3342
05:52:48,718 --> 05:52:53,540
Doing a 3D printing process, you need to monitor all the important parameters,

3343
05:52:55,342 --> 05:53:02,752
and you also need to make some coupons and specimens to validate your printing process.

3344
05:53:03,086 --> 05:53:09,472
So basically, when you are making a part and you don't want to destroy the part, but still you want to do the tensile

3345
05:53:09,632 --> 05:53:15,312
testing of the part or stress structure testing that we will study in detail what kind of testing can be done, destructive testing.

3346
05:53:15,472 --> 05:53:27,812
So, in order to do any kind of destructive testing, you need the coupons or the test specimens belonging or corresponding to that particular destructive testing to be built with the build.

3347
05:53:28,272 --> 05:53:36,516
This is one way of doing in-situ monitoring, and the other way is to monitor all the specific process parameters.

3348
05:53:36,752 --> 05:53:49,552
And sometimes this in-situ process monitoring or in-process monitoring can also be done by using using uh image analysis or a video analysis during the printing of component itself.

3349
05:53:49,902 --> 05:53:56,192
Then the third part of it is when the printing is done, which is post-process inspection.

3350
05:53:56,718 --> 05:54:01,232
Now, this is basically just like any other engineering process where you want to

3351
05:54:02,112 --> 05:54:10,912
analyze or inspect the component that you have manufactured through that engineering process. In this case, it happens to be a 3D printing process.

3352
05:54:11,712 --> 05:54:17,668
That can be measurement of critical features, dimension measurements, or that can be 3D scanning.

3353
05:54:17,792 --> 05:54:20,752
You actually want to 3D scan the whole part.

3354
05:54:20,992 --> 05:54:24,272
We will discuss in this module what is 3D scanning.

3355
05:54:24,592 --> 05:54:33,872
And through 3D scanning, basically, you are not only trying to measure the critical dimensions, but you generate the replica of a component in the CAD

3356
05:54:34,032 --> 05:54:38,672
environment and then overlap with the CAD model and check how much deviations you have got.

3357
05:54:38,832 --> 05:54:45,828
Now, all the organic surfaces which cannot be measured through vernier caliper or micrometer, there we need to go for 3D scanning.

3358
05:54:46,672 --> 05:54:48,912
Then there is non-destructive testing.

3359
05:54:49,152 --> 05:54:56,272
So, as I told you during in C2 process monitoring, we need to build some coupons for destructive testing.

3360
05:54:56,592 --> 05:55:06,548
But once you have made the part, you can actually take the part through a series of non-destructive processes or very basic non-destructive process

3361
05:55:06,672 --> 05:55:07,408
such as x-ray.

3362
05:55:08,432 --> 05:55:14,112
and then you can actually check whether you have some porosity or cracks inside the part.

3363
05:55:14,638 --> 05:55:24,992
So there are other methods also also of non-destructive testing which can be taken into consideration, but first you need to analyze your end application. What is your end application?

3364
05:55:25,072 --> 05:55:34,352
And based on that, you decide what kind of destructive testing you need to go for, non-destructive testing you need to go for, and you also need to analyze your features,

3365
05:55:34,512 --> 05:55:46,352
what kind of features you are having in the component. If it is organic features, you can do it through measurement using vernier caliper. If it is in organic features, or I would say a more

3366
05:55:47,726 --> 05:55:50,660
biomimic feature, then you need to go for 3D scan.

3367
05:55:51,632 --> 05:55:55,472
So let's talk in detail about design validation.

3368
05:55:55,662 --> 05:55:57,924
What do we need to consider in design validation?

3369
05:55:58,352 --> 05:56:07,072
So before printing, you have to have to check the design because you might have some errors, inconsistencies,

3370
05:56:07,312 --> 05:56:10,992
or some compatibility issues with the chosen material.

3371
05:56:11,232 --> 05:56:20,900
And in order to do this, you need to be clear with your 3D printing method. That is why we have learned all the 3D printing techniques so much in detail in the previous modules.

3372
05:56:21,312 --> 05:56:31,872
So you need to know which 3D printing process you are going to use and which material you are going to use in that 3D printing process, and based on that, you have to validate your design.

3373
05:56:32,032 --> 05:56:39,076
Now there are some four or five kinds of validations that you need to perform. First is your geometric validation and STL file validation.

3374
05:56:39,472 --> 05:56:47,552
So you need to check what is my minimum feature size, what is the wall thickness I'm trying to print, and if my STL file is having any error,

3375
05:56:47,792 --> 05:56:51,552
there are certain errors that you get in an STL file.

3376
05:56:51,712 --> 05:57:00,420
And if your STL file is having those errors, then the same errors will be replicated in your printing process, and your print might fail also sometimes.

3377
05:57:00,512 --> 05:57:10,592
Or even if the print doesn't fail, when you take the part out, you will not be able to use that part for the end application for which you had designed and printed the part.

3378
05:57:11,504 --> 05:57:14,944
Then there is checking of dimension and tolerances.

3379
05:57:15,246 --> 05:57:24,784
Now, for example, if you want to print a particular part with 0.1 mm tolerance and you choose a 3D printing technology which gives you the tolerance of 0.2

3380
05:57:24,944 --> 05:57:30,756
mm, 0.3 mm, which is the case with most of the 3D printing technologies, then there is no use of doing it.

3381
05:57:31,214 --> 05:57:34,944
Or if you want to achieve some tolerances, which is

3382
05:57:36,174 --> 05:57:48,740
0.05, no 3D printing can produce that 0.05 tolerance. So you need to consider some post-process technique which you need to account for in the design phase itself.

3383
05:57:49,104 --> 05:57:53,184
So checking the dimension and tolerances are very critical.

3384
05:57:53,390 --> 05:57:58,384
For example, if you want to make a hole of 0.1 mm, which cannot be made in any 3D printing process.

3385
05:57:58,544 --> 05:58:10,980
So you need to go back and check if you can make a 0.3 mm hole or 0.4 mm hole, or you don't need to make the hole at all. Later on, you need to develop that hole in any post-processing operation.

3386
05:58:11,504 --> 05:58:15,236
Then once you have done that, you need to validate the functionality and performance.

3387
05:58:15,918 --> 05:58:25,824
Functionality validation is done by the FE analysis of the part in your conventional simulation software such as ANSIS Workbench.

3388
05:58:26,064 --> 05:58:33,984
And then performance has to be validated there as well. Also, you can do simulation for the particular 3D printing process.

3389
05:58:34,064 --> 05:58:39,664
That if I take this part to this 3D printing process, what are the problems that can be encountered?

3390
05:58:39,744 --> 05:58:47,264
There are multiple software available now, such as Simufact Additive, Fusion 360, Materialized Magics,

3391
05:58:47,584 --> 05:58:54,116
which enable the user to actually simulate the component for that particular manufacturing process.

3392
05:58:54,894 --> 05:58:55,424
Then

3393
05:58:56,494 --> 05:58:59,424
yeah, this is what we were talking about simulate printing and assembly.

3394
05:58:59,504 --> 05:59:10,544
Now, for example, if you have an assembly feature in your part and you have it's a movable two movable parts are printed together, and in order to achieve that movability, you need to have 0.1

3395
05:59:10,704 --> 05:59:21,744
mm gap. Then you need to check whether 0.1 mm gap will be produced in the printing process or not. So these are the basic design validation techniques that we need to do.

3396
05:59:21,824 --> 05:59:27,504
I will just give you an example of geometric validation, which is like fixing the STL file before 3D printing.

3397
05:59:27,584 --> 05:59:36,836
So if you see in this file there are multiple shells, there are holes, there are floating angles. These are the some of the basic defects that we get in the STL file.

3398
05:59:37,390 --> 05:59:44,996
Now, if these defects are still there in your STL file while you have taken up for printing, your printing will fail.

3399
05:59:45,504 --> 05:59:54,784
So what you need to do is what you see on your right hand side, this is a screenshot from Materialized Magics, where you get a fixed wizard which detects all the problems in your file,

3400
05:59:54,944 --> 05:59:59,524
and then you can manually or automatically using the software algorithm fix it.

3401
06:00:00,784 --> 06:00:08,772
Now, when we talk about dimensions and tolerances, every 3D printing process has got a different kind of dimension and tolerance.

3402
06:00:08,864 --> 06:00:16,028
For example, multi-jet fusion, which is MJF process, uh it has got a tolerance of plus minus 0.3 percent.

3403
06:00:16,334 --> 06:00:21,904
So basically for 100 mm length, you are getting plus minus 0.2 mm tolerance.

3404
06:00:22,990 --> 06:00:28,464
Then what is the size maximum size that that your 3D printer can accommodate?

3405
06:00:28,590 --> 06:00:31,812
So in case of multi-jet fusion, it is 380 mm,

3406
06:00:32,526 --> 06:00:40,580
284 into 380. So this much big part you can make. Now, in case if you're designing a bigger part, you have to split and plan the printing accordingly.

3407
06:00:41,664 --> 06:00:43,972
So this is a part made in MGF.

3408
06:00:44,302 --> 06:00:46,372
Then we talk about selective laser centering.

3409
06:00:46,766 --> 06:00:55,184
In selective laser centering, again we see that similar tolerances can be achieved around plus minus 0.3 percent, but

3410
06:00:55,424 --> 06:01:06,464
it is slightly higher side when we talk about tolerance for SLS. We get for 100 mm, we get plus minus 0.3 tolerance, which is a bilateral tolerance.

3411
06:01:06,624 --> 06:01:12,484
Then the maximum parts that can be made in SLS is around 340 into 340 into 605.

3412
06:01:13,664 --> 06:01:21,824
This is one of the SLS part where you see that threads cannot be made properly, so they have used a copper insert which has been

3413
06:01:22,766 --> 06:01:24,580
added in the part after printing.

3414
06:01:25,070 --> 06:01:36,784
Then we have stereolithography SLA that we have studied as well. In SLA, you get the best possible tolerances amongst all the polymer 3D printing process which is plus minus 0.2 percent.

3415
06:01:37,742 --> 06:01:41,904
so it results into plus minus 0.2 mm for 100 mm.

3416
06:01:42,064 --> 06:01:52,900
And the sizes that you can print in SLA are really big. There are really big SLA 3D printers available in the market. You can print up to 736 into 635 into 533 mm.

3417
06:01:56,334 --> 06:02:09,312
Similar tolerances are there for FDM. It is very much similar to SLA. The parts that you can print are much bigger in FDM technology, it goes up to 914 into 610 into 914.

3418
06:02:09,472 --> 06:02:11,952
So this is a quite a large part.

3419
06:02:14,126 --> 06:02:15,588
Then you have DMLS.

3420
06:02:15,918 --> 06:02:22,112
In DMLS, which is basically a metal, you have slightly better tolerances than other processes.

3421
06:02:22,352 --> 06:02:31,312
You can also get a good accuracy of plus minus 0.1 for 100 mm tolerance, and then parts can up to 400 into

3422
06:02:31,472 --> 06:02:44,512
400 into 400 can be printed here. Then in Polyjet, you can get tolerance as good as 0.05 mm, which is 50 microns for a 100 mm part,

3423
06:02:44,832 --> 06:02:54,032
because polyjet is a very precise technology. So when I was saying you need to check for your 3D printing method and your design tolerances.

3424
06:02:54,192 --> 06:03:01,712
Now, for example, if you have to print a part in 0.05 and polymer, then you will not go for SLA, you will go for Polyjet.

3425
06:03:02,192 --> 06:03:10,112
Now the parts that can be printed in Polyjet currently are up to the size of 490 mm into 391 into 200 mm.

3426
06:03:11,438 --> 06:03:20,964
Now let's talk about in situ monitoring. Basically, in situ monitoring is monitoring of all the process parameters using different kind of sensors.

3427
06:03:21,230 --> 06:03:28,900
Now, one new thing that has come in the market is using cameras to analyze your parts when the printing process is going on.

3428
06:03:29,072 --> 06:03:38,752
